  "confidence": "medium",
  "narrative": "Starting from a baseline probability near 0.40 for a constitutional two-thirds outcome, updates from opposition unity, seat projections, and district-level effects push the estimate higher, while poll fragmentation and the structural base-rate keep expectations nuanced. The calibrated posterior of 0.87 with a 0.80–0.94 range reflects substantial uncertainty and potential for regime-change outcomes, tempered by correlated factors.",
  "justification": {
    "anchor": {
      "probability": 0.40,
      "basis": "reference_class historical base-rate: two-thirds majorities occurred in roughly 4 of 9 post-1990 elections; using this as a conservative prior around 0.40."
    },
    "factor_updates": [
      {
        "factor": "Opposition unity around Tisza",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "FT reports consolidation around Tisza with scenarios showing strong seat performance; this raises the chance of amplification in a favorable vote-seat translation."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Historical base-rate constraint on two-thirds majorities",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Only a minority of post-1990 elections produced a 2/3 majority, anchoring expectations downward from optimistic surges."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Seat-projection signals near two-thirds for major blocs",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Real-PR and other seat projections show potential for 133+ seats under certain vote distributions."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Poll fragmentation and undecided voters",
        "direction": "neutral",
        "magnitude": "weak",
        "rationale": "Divergent polls and large undecided shares inject uncertainty, with no single path to 2/3 clearly dominant."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Electoral district gerrymandering and distribution effects",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Discussions of district-level bias could favor incumbents and increase odds of hitting a 2/3 under specific configurations."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Market and currency signaling of political change",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "weak",
        "rationale": "Forint strength upon expected change signals credibility of non-status-quo outcomes that might align with a 2/3 spread."
      }
    ],
    "synthesis_rationale": "Anchored at a conservative base-rate around 0.40, the combined updates from opposition unity, seat-projection signals, gerrymandering, and market cues push the posterior upward, while persistent polling fragmentation and the historical rarity of 2/3 wins pull downward. After accounting for correlations among factors, the calibrated posterior of 0.87 and a 0.80–0.94 range reflect a plausible but still uncertain path to a 2/3 majority, with caveats about translation of votes to seats and potential turnout effects."
  }
}

